bill@polygen.uucp (Bill Poitras) writes:

>In article <91Feb28.200143est.2086@ois.db.toronto.edu> jdd@db.toronto.edu (John DiMarco) writes:
>>An intrinsic range of 1-99 makes a lot more sense than AD&D's confusing
>>3-18-18/** scheme. 

>Doesn't nethack have some basis in AD&D??

Yes. But it carries over some of AD&D's problems, including (IMHO) this one.

>>Why do barbarians have poison resistance? 

>AD&D (First Edition) Barbarians have a good Poison Resistance.  

"It's in AD&D" isn't a good enough justification, for me, if the feature
isn't coherent, realistic, or sensible. What is the justification for
a barbarian's poison resistance? His poorly-cooked food? :-)

>>Cavemen shouldn't have bows and arrows; they're supposed to be primitive,
>>neanderthal types, right? Leather armor is fine, a bunch of rocks for
>>throwing, a club, plus lots of strength and constitution (perhaps more
>>than they get currently).

>Maybe they're not as primitive as you think.

Are they primitive or are they not? If they're primitive, don't give them
high-quality weapons. If they're not primitive, don't call them cave-men.

>Again, another AD&D thing.  D&D Clerics are crusaders with a good ability
>to fight, with the mace being thier choice weapon.

True. But AD&D clerics don't really make all that much sense. Most clerics
tend not to be the crusader type. And crusaders, historically, mostly used 
swords, anyways.

>>Rogues are good. Give them a blessed spellbook of detect treasure on 
>>startup, though. 
>In D&D rogues don't get the ability to read spells until 10th level.
>Only an experienced thief should be able to understand arcane lore.  What
>I think rogues should have is picking locks, hiding in shadows (becoming
>invisible to monsters, though not to you), and the ability to pick
>pockets like a nymph steals stuff.

In nethack, all characters can cast spells right away, dependant on their
intelligence. Given this, a spellbook of detect treasure makes a lot of sense
in the hands of a greedy rogue.

Rogues already have stealth and a lock-pick. It would be nice for them
to be able to pick pockets, though, but it'd be more like a leprechaun's
ability to steal gold, rather than a nymph's ability to seduce-and-steal.

>>Dwarf: starts off with standard dwarven armor, pickaxe or 2-hand sword. 
>Very good idea.  I agree!

One of AD&D's better ideas...

>>Make dwarves "@" instead of "h".

>No.  I think that it would make the game more interesting as well as
>consistent to use an h.

If players can be dwarves, then dwarves are people, right? Then they should
be "@". If players can't be dwarves, then make them "h". 

In general, nethack shouldn't blindly copy ideas from AD&D. Examine each
idea: does it make sense? Is it consistent? Is it realistic? Some of AD&D's
ideas don't measure up.

John
